\title{ZK-Friendly Primitives}
\author{dhsorens}
\date{2026-08-18}

\p{
    The hash function is usually the prover's largest single cost, so primitive choice is a proof-system decision rather than an implementation detail.
}

\p{
    That framing is what the August 2026 decision to drop Poseidon for L1 inverts, and this page should be written with the reversal in view: [hash-friendly SNARKs rather than SNARK-friendly hashes](dhsorens-004D). The cost question below is still the right question — the answer simply stopped selecting for an exotic primitive.
}

\p{
    Questions this page should answer:
    \ul{
        \li{Arithmetisation-friendly hashes (Poseidon2 and relatives) against standard ones (Keccak, SHA-256, Blake3), counted in constraints.}
        \li{The security tradeoff: young algebraic designs against well-analysed bit-oriented ones.}
        \li{Where Ethereum has no choice — Keccak in the state trie, ECDSA in transactions — and what those cost.}
        \li{Hash rate as a headline benchmark, and what it does and does not predict.}
        \li{Which hash a [Merkle commitment](dhsorens-0031) should use at each layer of a recursion.}
    }
}
